<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial City of Besançon</h1><p class="st-meta">1290 CE – 1674 CE (approx.) · capital Besançon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A free city of the Holy Roman Empire inside Franche-Comté, ruled by its citizens rather than its archbishops until Louis XIV took it in 1674.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a> was an ancient archbishopric whose archbishops long held authority in the city. After repeated revolts, the citizens obtained recognition of their liberties, and in 1290 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> confirmed the city as a free imperial city, separate from the surrounding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Free County of Burgundy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was governed by elected governors and councillors drawn from its wards, while the archbishop kept spiritual and some ceremonial rights. It traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> valley. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-perrenot-de-granvelle">Nicolas Perrenot de Granvelle</a>, chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, built his palace there, and the city was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1654 the emperor ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, joining it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Franche-Comté</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took the city in 1674, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678 made it French. It became the capital of Franche-Comté, and Vauban built its citadel.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1290 CE – 1674 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.37%;width:7.64%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1290 CE – 1674 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Besançon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Besançon, Bisanz, Free Imperial City of Besançon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=imperial-city-of-besancon" aria-label="Imperial City of Besançon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4821 520 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial City of Besançon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5141 776L5229 694M5141 776L4910 989M5141 776L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M4910 989h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5141 776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5141 776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1482&amp;focus=imperial-city-of-besancon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
